{
  "course": "economic-growth",
  "title": "Economic Growth",
  "description": "Sixty original constructed exercises for an advanced undergraduate course with scaffolded graduate extensions. Undergraduate prerequisites: intermediate microeconomics and macroeconomics, algebra, logarithms and exponentials, single-variable calculus, probability, and introductory regression. Continuous-time questions supply their model equations and require interpreting growth rates and elementary differential equations. Graduate extensions in innovation valuation, semi-endogenous growth, allocation wedges and dynamic-panel identification benefit from optimization, stochastic processes and econometrics. The course omits repeated Ramsey and overlapping-generations derivations and focuses on long-run measurement, convergence, human capital, innovation and institutions. Numerical scenarios are hypothetical; official university materials inform coverage rather than supplying copied exercises or empirical claims.",
  "reviewStatus": "draft",
